### 2026-08-17T11:12:13.028Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Three concrete bugs fixed and committed:

1. **Scrub marker spanning everything** — the wrapper never actually got the `.notation-scroll` CSS class applied (I'd written the class but only used inline `overflow-x:auto`), so `position:relative` never took effect and the marker's absolute positioning escaped to the whole page. Fixed — it now stops exactly at the bottom of the stack.
2. **Doubling at measure 2** — measure 1 was being formatted against the same fixed width budget as later measures, but its clef/key/time-signature glyphs eat into that same space, leaving less real room for notes — so its last note overflowed past the barline into measure 2's territory. Now reads each stave's own actual usable width instead of guessing a constant.
3. **Ledger line / stem barely visible** — confirmed: VexFlow hardcodes ledger lines to `#444` and stems to `black` regardless of the render context's color, so my earlier dark-theme fix (which only covers context-inheriting elements) missed both. Now set explicitly per-note.

On the "play C, it doesn't go ahead" report: I reproduced actual gameplay both via direct calls and real keyboard input, and `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` advances correctly (C→D→E...) in both cases — the underlying logic isn't stuck. My guess is the visual bugs above (a full-page marker, overlapping noteheads) made it *look* broken even though it wasn't, and separately, the "3 clean reps before the drilled segment grows by a whole measure" mechanic is real but has zero visible indicator while it's counting — which likely explains the "sudden jump" too. Worth you retesting now that the visuals are fixed; if it's still stuck, that's a different, more specific bug I haven't found yet.

