### 2026-08-17T12:04:18.599Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

They're closely related but not quite interchangeable. **[REDACTED_STUDENT]** is the standard music-theory term for "octave-invariant note identity" — C, C#, D, etc., 12 of them, and it already includes the accidental (C and C# are different pitch classes). **[REDACTED_STUDENT]** is essentially a synonym for pitch class, more common in the music-psychology/MIR literature (from Shepard's "pitch = height + chroma" model) than in ordinary music theory — same concept, different field's vocabulary.

Neither term means quite what I wrote ("letter name alone") — that phrasing was imprecise on my part, since "E" without an accidental isn't really a named concept on its own (E and E♭ are different pitch classes/chroma but the same letter). Let me fix my draft: "pitch class" is the right term for what INV-25 is actually contrasting against full pitch (which adds octave). I'll use that instead of "letter name" throughout.


