### 2026-08-17T14:15:05.116Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Yes, but as stable IDs, not as an ordering signal. The numbers get referenced from test names (`"INV-4: ..."`), code comments (`// INV-46`), and cross-references between invariants themselves (`see INV-28`) — so keeping a given number attached to a given guarantee, even as it moves around in the file or gets merged/trimmed, is what makes those references still resolve correctly. That's why I moved INV-13 without renumbering it, and why merged-away numbers (46, 52) just vanish rather than shifting everything after them down.

What the numbers don't need to do is match file order — you just put 13 right after 1 despite the gap, and that's fine. If you'd rather they tracked position (i.e., renumber on move/merge), that's more churn for less benefit, since it'd break every existing cross-reference and test name on every reorder. I'd keep them as IDs unless you're seeing a real cost from the gaps.


