### 2026-08-19T03:44:02.818Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Confirmed — it *is* the same shared implementation. `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` and the base `.timeline-marker` CSS are identical for both modes (same full-height, same drag mechanics). There's exactly one deliberate override, scoped to Compose only:

```css
[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO] {
    [REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]
    height: 1.2em; /* matches .pitch-row's own height */
}
```

The reason: Compose's staff is itself directly editable — click empty space to add a note, drag a notehead to re-pitch it. If the marker spanned the full staff height (like Melody's do), it would sit on top of the staff at the same x-position and silently swallow clicks meant for a notehead instead of letting the staff's own click/drag handler receive them. That's a real bug that was found live ("a click landed on the marker, not the notehead, exactly where a fresh piece's default markers and its first note coincide"), not a design that was never considered — the CSS comment documents it directly. Melody's staff has no competing click target (you can't click-edit notes on Melody's staff), so its markers keep the tall, easy-to-grab full-stack span.

So: same component, one small height override, for a genuine interaction conflict unique to Compose.


