### 2026-08-20T07:34:26.600Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

All three, precisely traced:

**15→16 and 31→32 (the block-of-6/block-of-3 splits, and the left gap)**: Both are the same phenomenon, and it's exactly what "piece-origin rigidity" is designed to do. What looks like one contiguous block of 6 is actually *multiple separate pieces* that happen to be touching — each keeps falling according to its own anchor and its own independent left/right slide choice when blocked, not as one welded mass. That's deliberate: it's the fix from a few sessions ago (pieces that merely touch stay independent; only cells that fell together as one piece stay rigid together). The alternative — welding touching pieces into one group once they come to rest — would bring back the freeze bug we spent real effort fixing, since a big welded mass can get permanently stuck the instant any one part of it touches something. So this is a real tradeoff, not an oversight. If you want, I can look at a narrower version — e.g., only weld pieces together at the moment they *first come to rest* touching each other (not while both are still independently falling) — but that's a design change worth deciding on deliberately, not something to just do.

**28→29**: This one's a genuinely different mechanism, and I'm glad you flagged it — it wasn't shape-matching at all. The locked piece was the `I`-piece lying along the diagonal axis (`[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]-(-1,3)-(-2,4)-(-3,5)`), and it contributed exactly **one** cell, `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`, which happened to be the single missing cell completing row q=2. That one clear let everything above resettle — which completed row q=1 on the very next tick, which triggered another resettle that completed row q=0 the tick after. Three full-line clears, cascading, from one 4-cell placement — only the first was the piece's own doing; the other two were emergent consequences of debris falling into place afterward. The "straight piece filling a crooked fissure" you saw was mostly that cascade, not the piece's own shape conforming to anything crooked.


