### 2026-08-20T07:42:52.528Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

That framing is a good one — and honestly, "weld at rest" turns out to be the safer building block for both ideas, not just an alternative to static-cling: since a group only ever gets fragmented correctly via `[REDACTED_SECRET]`'s connectivity check (already proven safe by the freeze fix), welding two genuinely-touching, already-resting groups into one is symmetric and low-risk — a later clear cutting through the merged mass still resplits it correctly, same as any other piece. Static cling is the more interesting idea but a deeper change: right now there's no real "choice" node in the code, just one primary offset and one blocked-fallback offset picked deterministically by parity — giving debris an actual preference between two open paths means generalizing that fallback to evaluate both directions when both are open, which is more surgery on the core fall logic.

My recommendation: build "weld at rest" first — it's the smaller, well-scoped piece, reuses infrastructure you already trust, and is naturally difficulty-gateable (easy welds, hard never does) the same way Blast/Gravity's piece-size difficulty already works. Static cling is worth keeping on the list as a v2 once weld-at-rest's difficulty-tunable framing is in place, since "prefer contact" is really the *soft* version of the same knob.

Not proposing we implement either right now — just flagging that's where I'd start if/when you want to move on this.


