To make sure I build the right thing — my read of the design:

**In `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`** (real gameplay code, since this replaces the fragile parallel-`setTimeout` flourish with something deterministic and replay-safe):
- Replace `playFlourish()`'s "N independent `setTimeout`s fired all at once + 1 finish timeout" with a **self-chaining** sequence: play step 0's note, `Replay.recordTick()`, schedule step 1 via a single `setTimeout(..., 100)`, and so on — one timeout alive at a time, not N stacked ones. This is also what fixes the fragility you flagged (a chain can't drift/overlap the way N independently-scheduled timeouts can).
- Reuse `tick()`/`Replay.recordTick()` as the *one* uniform "automatic advance" entry point: instead of early-returning while `isFlourishing`, `tick()` performs one flourish step. That means the replay tool needs **zero changes** to its existing tick-count catch-up loop — `SnakeMode.tick()` called N times already replays flourish steps and movement ticks alike, since they now share the same counter and entry point.
- During a flourish, the driving interval runs at the fixed 100ms note-spacing (not `state.speed`), so real play still sounds the same; it hands back to the normal movement interval once the last step's tail pause elapses.
