Answering directly:

1. **Correct on both counts.** This whole workflow only makes sense for developers running their own local server — a deployed `github.io` build has no `replays/` directory serving real files at all (it's `.gitignore`d), and there's no live game-state introspection to replay against remotely anyway. This is a local dev tool, full stop.

2. **Yes**, `http-server` (what `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]`'s `webServer` already standardizes on) auto-generates a directory listing when a folder has no `index.html` — I just confirmed it directly: `[REDACTED_PRIVATE_INFO]` returns a real `<table>` with one `<a href="./filename.json">filename.json</a>` row per file.

3. There's a wrinkle this reveals, more important than version-to-version formatting drift: **the moment I put our own `index.html` in `replays/`, `http-server` stops generating that auto-listing entirely** and serves our file instead (standard static-server behavior — an existing `index.html` always wins). So a page *named* `index.html` in that folder can't fetch-and-parse "the directory listing," because there won't be one anymore; it would just be fetching itself.

That changes the design: I'd put the browsing UI at a **different filename** (e.g. `replays/browse.html`), leaving `replays/` itself index-less so `http-server`'s real listing keeps working and picks up new files automatically with zero rebuild step. `browse.html` fetches `/replays/`, parses out the `<a href="...">` entries ending in `.json` (a link-per-file table is close to universal across static-server autoindexers, not an `http-server`-specific quirk), and lists them.

That handles *discovery* for free, with the "just drop a file" UX intact. The bigger open question is what happens on click: pre-generate a `.viewer.html` (needs Playwright, i.e. me, to run first — not what you want) versus a genuinely **live, in-browser replay engine** that loads the real app and drives it from the JSON directly, no generation step ever. The second is more work but matches "click to replay it on demand" literally. Want me to go that route?


