### 2026-08-26T15:54:46.068Z | claude (claude-sonnet-5) | claude | conversation 627637ca-d250-4ad7-8a24-ddd841e171dd

Found a real, fundamental divergence — not a subtle drift like before. Tracing tick-by-tick from the very first reset: the snake starts at center heading right, gets **zero steering input for 11 real ticks**, and (per Snake's own deterministic physics — straight line from center, radius-7 arena) dies hitting the wall at **[REDACTED_SECRET]**, before the first recorded direction press even lands. This isn't a bug in the reconstruction; it's mathematically exact given what's in the log. But the real session reached score 17 in that same game, so somewhere in those first 11 ticks, real steering happened that never made it into the recorded event log.

My best explanation, consistent with the same class of gap found in the earlier desktop 2-game Snake investigation: a touch-**slide** gesture across the D-pad (finger moving from one button's area to another without lifting) can trigger each button's own `ontouchstart` handler as it's crossed, but the app's replay recorder listens for `pointerdown` at the document level — which only fires once, at the start of the gesture. A real player sliding across multiple direction buttons in one continuous motion could steer successfully many times while only one `pointerdown` ever gets logged.

Given this diverges within the first 8 ticks of game 1 (not just game 2 like the desktop session), essentially none of this specific recording is faithfully replayable — I don't think it's usable as a story test as-is, and forcing one through would mean asserting on an outcome that doesn't match what actually happened, same mistake I avoided last time. Marking that task blocked rather than faking it, and moving on to the four bug fixes now as you asked — I'll flag this gap separately once I'm through those, since it looks like a real, fixable recording bug in its own right (touch-slide gestures not being captured), distinct from the story test itself.


